=== task: mcq medium
title: Dijkstra's greedy choice
prompt: Dijkstra's algorithm is a greedy algorithm. Which choice does it make greedily and never undo?
* Settling the unsettled vertex with the smallest best-known distance, whose distance is then final :: Right. That's its greedy step, and the proof that a settled distance is final is its exchange argument.
- Following the shortest edge out of the vertex it's at :: That's a different rule, and it can fail: a short first edge can lead on to long ones.
- Visiting the vertices in the order they're listed in the graph :: The order depends on the distances found, not on how the graph is written.
- Lowering a vertex's best-known distance when an edge offers a shorter route :: That's relaxing an edge. It can happen several times to the same vertex before it's settled, so it isn't a final choice.
hint: What does Dijkstra do with the vertex that comes off the heap?
hint: Which decision is never changed afterwards?
right: Each round settles the nearest unsettled vertex for good. Because no length is negative, no later route to it can be shorter, which is what makes this greedy choice safe.
wrong: Dijkstra's greedy choice is to settle the nearest unsettled vertex: its distance is final from then on.

=== task: predict medium
title: Fewest arrows
prompt: Balloons are stretched along a wall, each from a start position to an end position. An arrow fired at position x bursts every balloon with start ≤ x ≤ end. This greedy finds the fewest arrows: it goes through the balloons in order of end, and when the last arrow fired misses a balloon, it fires a new one at that balloon's end. Where are the arrows fired?
